        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix(agent-core): bound harness exec stdout/stderr at 16 MiB",
        "body": "## What Problem This Solves\n\nFixes unbounded stdout/stderr string accumulation in the agent harness `NodeExecutionEnv.exec()` method. Child process output is accumulated into `stdout += chunk` / `stderr += chunk` with no byte cap, so a runaway command producing large output can exhaust the agent process heap (OOM). This affects every agent exec call through `packages/agent-core`.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\nAdd a `MAX_EXEC_OUTPUT_BYTES = 16 MiB` cap with a `maxOutputBytes` option so callers can override. When output exceeds the cap, the stream is truncated, `[output truncated]` is appended, and the process is killed. The 16 MiB default matches the existing SSH sandbox output cap. No API break — existing call sites work unchanged with the default cap.\n\n## User Impact\n\nRunaway commands that dump unbounded output (e.g. `cat /dev/urandom`, infinite loops) can no longer OOM the agent process. Normal commands with moderate output are unaffected. Callers can raise or lower `maxOutputBytes` per invocation.\n\n## Evidence\n\n### Vitest regression tests (real child process spawn)\n\n```\npnpm test packages/agent-core/src/harness/env/nodejs.test.ts\n```\n\n```\n Test Files  1 passed (1)\n      Tests  7 passed (7)\n```\n\n- **under-cap**: `printf \"hello\"` with 1 KiB cap → full output, no truncation\n- **stdout truncation**: 10 KB stdout with 1 KiB cap → truncated, contains `[output truncated]`\n- **stderr truncation**: 10 KB stderr with 1 KiB cap → truncated\n- **no-op**: 500 bytes with 64 KiB cap → full output, no truncation\n- **existing tests**: timeout resolution unchanged (3 tests)\n\n### Negative control\n\nReverted stdout handler to `origin/main` (unbounded `stdout += chunk`):\n\n```\n FAIL  truncates stdout when output exceeds maxOutputBytes\nAssertionError: expected 10002 to be less than or equal to 1200\n```\n\nOld code accumulates all 10 KB without truncation — confirming the unbounded accumulation.\n\n### What was not tested\n\nThe 16 MiB cap was not exercised at full scale (truncation tested at 1 KiB). The mechanism is byte-count-based using `Buffer.byteLength(chunk, \"utf8\")` and scales identically at any cap value.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)",
